    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "2002.108.020 a",
      "Vonž¾(y\nNC53\nFiller\nPan American 's fist passenger flghe Key Mest, Ferida, lo Havana, Cula, January 16, 1928\nFřikker Fı",
      "Historic First Flights of Pan American Clippers\nPan American's first passenger flight\nKey Nest, Morida, to Havana, Cuba,\nJanuary 16, 1928\nSeven passengers board a Pan American Fokker F-7 (NC53), inaugurating scheduled United States-flag\ncommercial passenger service between Key West and Havana, a distance of 90 miles. The trimotor, high-wing\nmonoplane was commanded by Captain Hugh I. Wells. A sister ship flown by Captain Edwin C. Musick took off a\nfew minutes earlier, carrying mail and cargo to Havana.\nThis passenger flight was one of Pan American's many aviation 'firsts!' It was preceded by the inauguration\nof airmail service to Havana on October 28, 1927, Pan Am's first flight and the first scheduled international flight\nby an American airline.\nAbout the Artist\nIn 1926, John T. McCoy became a working student for the Curtiss Flying Ser\nvice, Curtiss Field, Long Island, New York.\nWhen Charles A. Lindbergh arrived at Curtiss Field, his SPIRIT OF ST. LOUIS\nwas housed in one of the Curtiss hangers. It was at this time that McCoy met\nLindbergh, who was most cordial and friendly to those who worked on the flight\nline. In those days muscle power was the method used for starting engines, ac-\ncomplished by swinging the propeller by hand. McCoy often pulled a prop through\nYears later, when McCoy was commissioned to do paintings depicting the\nhistory of Pan American World Airways, Inc. - entitled HISTORIC FIRST FLIGHTS\nOF PAN AMERICAN CLIPPERS - he consulted with Lindbergh on the selection of\naircraft and locations for the series.\nThis is one picture in a series of thirteen. If you would like to purchase 8½\"x 11\"\nor 16\" x 20\" limited edition prints, send your letter of inquiry to: Public Relations\nDepartment - Pan American World Airways, Inc., Pan Am Building, New York, New\nYork 10166. Please include code number 304 in your letter.",
      "Martini \u0026 Rossi Dry Vermouth\nAperitifs\nDry Sack\nHarvey Bristol Cream Campari\nManhattan\nBloody Mary\nJ. Walker Black\nCanadian Club\nCocktails\nVodka Martini\nGin Martini\nWhiskey Sour\nScrewdriver\nDaiquiri\nTom Collins\nVodka Collins\nSpirits\nJ\u0026 B Scotch\nSmirnoff Vodka\nOld Grand Dad\nJack Daniels\nBeefeater Gin\nBacardi\nVines\nBuena Vista Chardonnay\nBuena Vista Cabernet Sauvignon\nChampagne\nFreixenet Cordon Negro\nLiqueurs \u0026 Cognac\nBénédictine D.O.M. Drambuie\nKahlúa\nGrand Marnier\nAmaretto di Saronno\nCognac Camus Napoléon\nBeverages\nCoca-Cola\nTab\nCanada Dry\nAssorted Beers\nFruit Juices",
      "Fioria Fine Flighs ol\nasherinal",
      "Menu\nCaviar Garni\nFresh Caviar served with traditional accompaniments of hard cooked egg,\nonion, sour cream, toast triangles and blinis\nHors d'Oeuvre Variés\nSelected Hors d'Oeuvres\nSalade Verte Mêlée\nCaesar Salad presented from the Dining Service Cart\nRôti au Vol\nChateaubriand roasted on board and carved to order. Served with an assortment of sauces\nCôtes de Veau à la Hongroise\nSautéed veal chops in a rich brandy cream sauce\nSole en Croûte\nFilets of Dover sole with a crabmeat filling baked in puff pastry\nPommes Dauphine\nDauphine Potatoes\nBouquetière de Légumes\nau Beurre\nButtered Vegetable Bouquet\nNouilles au Beurre\nButtery Egg Noodles\nFruits et Fromages\nFresh Fruit and Selected Cheeses\nSoufflé Grand Marnier\nGrand Marnier Souffle\nou\nCoupe Glacée\nIce Cream Sundae\nCate\nCoffee\nCafé Décaféiné\nSanka\nThé\nTea\nPlease accept our apology if, due to previous passenger selections, your choice is not available.\n289K\n1-15-83"
